/-! # Cartier boundary of the saturation of an affine open

Source: Brion, Algebraic group actions on normal varieties, arXiv:1703.09506v3,
Section 3.2, proof of Theorem 1 and the Cartier part of Proposition 3.3.
The extension to disconnected normal schemes is the one specified in the DAG.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-affine-saturation-cartier-boundary)

Internal group and module objects over Spec k express the group scheme and its
scheme-level action. An equivariant open immersion with the prescribed image
expresses the open saturation with its restricted action. The divisor's actual
quasi-coherent kernel ideal is required to be locally generated by a non-zero-divisor.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits CategoryTheory.MonoidalCategory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The saturation of an affine open has an effective Cartier boundary.

Source: Brion, Algebraic group actions on normal varieties, arXiv:1703.09506v3,
Section 3.2, proof of Theorem 1 and Proposition 3.3, first proof paragraph.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-affine-saturation-cartier-boundary)

In the image formula, `((toUnit X.X ≫ g) • 𝟙 X.X).left` is the scheme morphism
x ↦ a(g,x), by the internal action on Hom sets. The source of g is Spec k.
The open immersion identifies W with the open subscheme on precisely that union.
Its equivariance states the factorization of the original action through W;
the module structure on W includes the restricted identity and associativity laws.
The ideal `d.ker` is the ideal of the closed immersion, not a chosen ideal with
only the same support. Empty U, W and D are allowed.

Proof, following the accepted eleven-step split:
1. The translates $gU$ are open: the action identities give mutually inverse morphisms $a_g$ and
   $a_{g^{-1}}$. Their union $W$ is therefore an open subscheme. If $U$ is empty then $W$ and
   its divisor are empty; henceforth allow this case by the same constructions. The field
   spectrum is Noetherian (a field has only the ideals $0,1$), so [lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-
   over-noetherian] makes $X$ and $G$ Noetherian. By [lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-open-subscheme-
   noetherian], $W$ and $U$ are Noetherian, in particular quasi-compact. By [lem-sfb-noetherian-
   open-closed-finite-presentation] (take the closed immersion to be the identity),
   $W\hookrightarrow X$ is quasi-compact; [lem-weil-pbc-qc-open-immersion-finite-type] and [lem-
   sfb-finite-type-composition-base-change] make $W$ of finite type over $k$. The latter node
   also makes products of these finite-type $k$-schemes finite type. The local rings on $W$
   equal those on $X$, hence are normal. Its relative diagonal is the restriction of the closed
   diagonal of $X/k$ to $W\times_kW$, so $W/k$ is separated. By [lem-sc-separated-over-affine-
   scheme-separated], $W$ is also absolutely separated.
2. The equality $hW=W$ holds for every $h\in G(k)$, since $a_h(gU)=(hg)U$. To upgrade this to
   scheme-level invariance, let $C=a^{-1}(X\setminus W)\cap(G\times_kW)$, a closed subset of the
   finite-type scheme $G\times_kW$. If nonempty, [lem-sc-lft-over-field-jacobson-space] supplies
   a closed ambient point in $C$, and [lem-kun-closed-point-field](a) makes its residue field
   finite over $k$. Since $k$ is algebraically closed, that field is $k$, giving a pair
   $(h,w)\in G(k)\times W(k)$. But $hw\in W$ by the preceding equality, a contradiction. Thus
   $C$ is empty. By [lem-weil-pbc-morphism-factors-through-open-subscheme] the action restricts
   to $\alpha:G\times_kW\to W$. Its identity and associativity identities follow after composing
   with the open immersion $W\to X$; that immersion is a monomorphism by [lem-sfb-noetherian-
   open-closed-finite-presentation].
3. The group $G$ is normal and reduced by [lem-trf-smooth-geometrically-normal], applied to its
   smooth structure map. Every quasi-compact open of $G$ is Noetherian by [lem-weil-pbc-
   noetherian-open-subscheme-noetherian], and has finitely many irreducible components by [lem-
   weil-pbc-noetherian-finitely-many-components]. Consequently [lem-sfb-normal-locally-finite-
   components] makes the irreducible components of $G$ pairwise disjoint and clopen.
   Connectedness and the identity point force there to be exactly one. This component, with its
   reduced induced structure, is $G$ itself. The geometric-irreducibility clause of [lem-sfb-
   group-local-minimal-prime] now shows that $G_K$ is irreducible for every field extension
   $K/k$.
4. Write $p:G\times_kW\to W$ for the second projection. The structure morphism
   $G\to\operatorname{Spec}k$ is affine: the field spectrum has only the opens $\varnothing$ and
   itself, with affine inverse images. It is therefore separated and quasi-compact by [lem-weil-
   pbc-affine-morphism-permanence](3), and its smoothness gives local finite presentation.
   Together these are finite presentation. The base-change clauses of [lem-pc-proper-base-
   change], [lem-weil-pbc-separated-base-change] and [lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence]
   show that $p$ is smooth, separated and finitely presented. The morphisms $b(g,y)=(g,gy)$ and
   $c(g,z)=(g,g^{-1}z)$ are mutually inverse by the action identities, and $\alpha=p\circ b$.
   Hence $\alpha$ is smooth by [lem-weil-pbc-smooth-restrict-open-subschemes](c). Its
   separatedness and finite presentation follow as well: the diagonal squares and affine
   presentations are carried by this isomorphism to those of $p$.
5. Choose any affine open $T\subseteq W$ from [lem-weil-pbc-affine-opens-basis], and set
   $Y_T=\alpha^{-1}(T)$ and $V_T=Y_T\cap(G\times_kU)$. The morphism $f_T:Y_T\to T$ is the base
   change of $\alpha$, hence smooth, separated and finitely presented by the three base-change
   results in step 4. The base $T$ is normal and locally Noetherian because its stalks are those
   of $W$ and it is an open of a Noetherian scheme. By [lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-
   spectra], $G\times_kU$ is affine. Its action morphism to the absolutely separated $W$ is
   affine by [lem-weil-pbc-affine-source-separated-target-affine-morphism]. Its inverse image of
   $T$, which is exactly $V_T$, is therefore affine by the definition [pqf-def-affine-morphism].
6. For any $t\in T$, with residue field $K=\kappa(t)$, the formula $g\mapsto(g,g^{-1}t)$
   identifies $(Y_T)_t$ with $G_K$; its inverse is first projection. By step 3 this fibre is
   irreducible. There is $h\in G(k)$ with $t\in hU$, directly from the definition of $W$ (no
   closedness of $t$ is required). The canonical $K$-point $t:\operatorname{Spec}K\to W$ then
   has $h^{-1}t$ in $U$, by the open factorization criterion used in step 2. Thus $(h,h^{-1}t)$
   is a $K$-point of $(V_T)_t$. This fibre is a nonempty open in an irreducible fibre, so it is
   dense: any two nonempty opens in an irreducible space intersect.
7. Apply [lem-sfb-smooth-normal-affine-boundary-cartier] to $f_T:Y_T\to T$ and the affine open
   $V_T$. The base is affine normal locally Noetherian, the map is smooth separated of finite
   presentation, and step 6 verifies density in every fibre. We obtain an effective Cartier
   divisor $E_T=(Y_T\setminus V_T)_{\mathrm{red}}$ on $Y_T$. This is the smooth-family Cartier
   criterion, rather than any assertion about ampleness or quasi-projectivity.
8. Let $D=(W\setminus U)_{\mathrm{red}}$ be the reduced induced closed subscheme furnished by
   [lem-weil-pbc-reduced-induced-closed-subscheme], with its quasi-coherent ideal $\mathcal
   I_D$. It is locally Noetherian: its closed immersion is proper by [lem-weil-pbc-proper-fp-
   permanence](c), hence of finite type; composing with $W\to\operatorname{Spec}k$ by [lem-sfb-
   finite-type-composition-base-change] and applying [lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian]
   even makes $D$ Noetherian. The projection $G\times_kD\to D$ is smooth by [lem-pc-proper-base-
   change]. Since $D$ is reduced, [lem-trf-smooth-reduced-dense-pullback] makes $G\times_kD$
   reduced. Its immersion into $G\times_kW$ is closed by base change of $D\hookrightarrow W$,
   using [lem-weil-pbc-proper-fp-permanence](b).
9. Put $B=G\times_kD=p^{-1}(D)$ as a closed subscheme. On $Y_T$ its underlying closed set is
   $Y_T\setminus V_T$, since $D$ has complement $U$. Both $B|_{Y_T}$ and $E_T$ are reduced;
   uniqueness in [lem-weil-pbc-reduced-induced-closed-subscheme](3) identifies their ideal
   sheaves, including their inclusions in $\mathcal O_{Y_T}$. The opens $Y_T$ cover $G\times_kW$
   as $T$ runs through the affine opens of $W$. Therefore the actual ideal of $B$ is locally
   generated by a non-zero-divisor: this holds on each $Y_T$ by step 7. Thus $B\hookrightarrow
   G\times_kW$ is an effective Cartier divisor. This identifies closed subschemes and ideals,
   not just supports.
10. The singleton $\{p:G\times_kW\to W\}$ is an fpqc covering in [def-weil-pbc-fpqc-covering].
   Indeed $p$ is flat by [lem-pc-smooth-flat]. For every affine open $T\subseteq W$, its inverse
   image $G\times_kT$ is affine by [lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra], and maps onto
   $T$: the identity section $t\mapsto(e,t)$ gives a preimage for every $t$. Thus one affine
   open in the covering source suffices for each affine target open, exactly as required in that
   definition.
11. Apply [lem-trf-fpqc-cartier-ideal-reflection] to $D\hookrightarrow W$, its quasi-coherent
   ideal $\mathcal I_D$, and the fpqc cover $p$. Its pulled-back closed subscheme is exactly
   $B$, whose ideal is effective Cartier by step 9. The cited result identifies $p^*\mathcal
   I_D$ with that ideal and concludes that $\mathcal I_D$ itself is an effective Cartier ideal.
   Hence $D$ is the required effective Cartier divisor with $|D|=|W|\setminus|U|$. The unit
   ideal is allowed, so this includes $U=W$ and the empty saturation. No component of $X$
   omitted by $U$ has been introduced into $W$, and the proof has not assumed that $X$ is
   integral or smooth.
-/
theorem exists_affine_saturation_cartier_boundary
    (k : Type u) [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k]
    (G : Grp (Over (Spec (.of k))))
    [Smooth G.X.hom] [ConnectedSpace G.X.left] [IsAffine G.X.left]
    [LocallyOfFiniteType G.X.hom] [QuasiCompact G.X.hom]
    (X : CategoryTheory.Mod (Over (Spec (.of k))) G.X)
    [IsSeparated X.X.hom] [LocallyOfFiniteType X.X.hom] [QuasiCompact X.X.hom]
    (hnormal : ∀ p : X.X.left,
      IsDomain (X.X.left.presheaf.stalk p) ∧
        IsIntegrallyClosed (X.X.left.presheaf.stalk p))
    (U : X.X.left.Opens) (hU : IsAffineOpen U) :
    ∃ (W : CategoryTheory.Mod (Over (Spec (.of k))) G.X) (i : W ⟶ X),
      IsOpenImmersion i.hom.left ∧
      Set.range i.hom.left =
        ⋃ g : (𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of k))) ⟶ G.X),
          (((CartesianMonoidalCategory.toUnit X.X ≫ g) • 𝟙 X.X).left) ''
            (U : Set X.X.left) ∧
      (∀ p : W.X.left, IsDomain (W.X.left.presheaf.stalk p) ∧
        IsIntegrallyClosed (W.X.left.presheaf.stalk p)) ∧
      IsSeparated W.X.hom ∧ LocallyOfFiniteType W.X.hom ∧ QuasiCompact W.X.hom ∧
      CompactSpace W.X.left ∧
      ∃ (D : Scheme.{u}) (d : D ⟶ W.X.left),
        IsClosedImmersion d ∧ d.ker.IsInvertible ∧
          Set.range d = {w : W.X.left | i.hom.left w ∉ U} := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
